  "query_preview": "..Morning satellite imagery reveals an apparent\ncirculation shifting across Utah, associated with a mid-level\nclosed low that brought scattered light showers to the area. As \nthis low continues to track eastward, these showers will do the \nsame, largely ending by midnight tonight. Low-level clouds are \nlikely to linger into the morning hours across much of the area, \npotentially inhibiting fog potential even for fog-prone locations\nsuch as the Cache Valley. \n\nA weak shortwave trough moving through the area late Thursday\nnight/early Friday morning is trending stronger with recent model\nruns, though moisture will be very limited, even moreso than\ntoday. Still, isolated showers may develop across far northeastern\nUtah/southwest Wyoming Thursday night; sometimes, a vort max will\nprovide enough lift to produce showers despite this lack of\nmoisture. Increased cloud cover, alongside slightly better mixing,\nmay once again inhibit valley fog development early Friday\nmorning, something that looked more likely in prior forecasts.\nFinally, this wave will also act to keep temperatures around 5 \ndegrees below normal through Friday.\n\nHigh pressure will build heading into the weekend, resulting in a\nsteady warming trend that will bring temperatures closer to 5-10\ndegrees above normal by early next week. Forecast confidence is\nlow in our next precipitation chances; model guidance suggests a\nreturn as early as next Thursday, but likely not until Friday or\nlater.",
